 # Lighting and Shading Models
+
+Also called: SDF Shading, Raymarched Lighting
+
 ## Overview
-Lighting and Shading Models compute the final color of a surface point identified by distance-field ray marching by combining geometric properties (normals, depth) with light source parameters [G]. This family solves the problem of transforming raw geometric hits into visually plausible images by simulating how light interacts with surfaces [G]. It is distinct from [Distance-field ray marching](21-distance-field-ray-marching.md) (which finds *where* the surface is) and [Volumetric and Atmospheric Rendering](4968-volumetric-and-atmospheric-rendering.md) (which computes light transport *through* participating media), focusing instead on surface reflectance and local illumination effects [G].
+Lighting and Shading Models compute the color of surface points identified by distance-field ray marching by estimating surface normals and applying reflectance models to determine how light interacts with the geometry [S1, unofficial]. This family solves the problem of shading ray-marched geometry without explicit mesh topology, distinguishing itself from [Distance Color Blending](791-distance-color-blending.md) by calculating geometric lighting contributions (diffuse/specular) rather than relying solely on distance-based color mapping, and from [Ray-marched Shadows](1087-ray-marched-shadows.md) by focusing on local reflectance properties rather than global occlusion calculations.
 
 ## Shared principles
 All methods in this family operate on a surface point $\mathbf{p}$ and its associated geometric data, primarily the surface normal $\mathbf{n}$ [G]. The normal is typically derived from the Signed Distance Function (SDF) $f(\mathbf{x})$ using the gradient:
 $$ \mathbf{n} = \frac{\nabla f(\mathbf{p})}{\|\nabla f(\mathbf{p})\|} $$
-where $\nabla f(\mathbf{p})$ is estimated via finite differences or analytical derivatives [S1, unofficial].
