 ### [Distance Color Blending](791-distance-color-blending.md)
-Enhances depth perception by blending sampled voxel colors with a background color based on distance from the viewer, solving the foreground/background distinction problem in dense volumetric data by applying a non-linear transparency falloff. It is distinct from [Ray-marched Shadows](1087-ray-marched-shadows.md) (which occlude light) and [Ambient Occlusion via SDF](1631-ambient-occlusion-via-sdf.md) (which darkens crevices), as it specifically targets global depth cues rather than local geometry lighting. Choose it for simulating atmospheric haze or aerial perspective [G].
+[Distance Color Blending](791-distance-color-blending.md) enhances depth perception by blending sampled voxel colors with a background color based on distance from the viewer. It solves the foreground/background distinction problem in dense volumetric data by applying a non-linear transparency falloff that dims distant objects and shifts their hue, simulating atmospheric haze or aerial perspective. Unlike standard lighting models that compute surface reflectance, this method operates on the accumulated color along the ray, modulating it with depth.
 
 ### [Ray-marched Shadows](1087-ray-marched-shadows.md)
-Determines if a surface point is occluded from a light source by performing sphere tracing along a ray directed from the surface point toward the light, returning a binary occlusion factor. It differs from screen-space shadow maps by leveraging the global geometric information inherent in the SDF, requiring no separate shadow map pass or rasterization overhead. Choose it for hard, accurate shadows in SDF-based scenes [G].
+[Ray-marched Shadows](1087-ray-marched-shadows.md) determine if a surface point is occluded from a light source by performing a sphere trace (ray march) from the point towards the light. Unlike rasterization-based shadow maps, this method computes occlusion directly from the Signed Distance Function (SDF) of the scene, producing alias-free hard shadows with a single additional traversal per light. It sets itself apart from [Soft Shadow Ray Marching](1107-soft-shadow-ray-marching.md) by returning a binary occlusion result rather than a continuous penumbra factor, and from [Shadow Casting Algorithm](1657-shadow-casting-algorithm.md) by operating in image-order on surface points rather than projecting light through a volumetric dataset.
 
 ### [Ambient Occlusion via SDF](1631-ambient-occlusion-via-sdf.md)
-Approximates the darkening of crevices and contact points by sampling the SDF along the surface normal, detecting occlusion by comparing the actual distance to nearby geometry against the expected distance if the surface were isolated. Unlike screen-space methods or shadow maps, this technique requires no separate rendering pass or auxiliary buffers, relying solely on the scene's distance function. Choose it for computationally cheaper local approximation of global illumination effects [G].
+[Ambient Occlusion via SDF](1631-ambient-occlusion-via-sdf.md) approximates the darkening of crevices and contact points by sampling the signed distance function (SDF) along the surface normal. It detects occlusion by comparing the actual distance to nearby geometry against the expected distance if the surface were isolated; a smaller actual distance indicates an obstacle is closer, reducing the ambient light contribution. Unlike screen-space methods or shadow maps, this technique requires no separate rendering pass, relying solely on the scene's distance function sampled differently. It is computationally cheaper than full ray-marched shadows but provides a local approximation of global illumination effects.
 
