+### [Multi-Layer Shading / OmniShader](2511-multi-layer-shading-omnishader.md)
+[Multi-Layer Shading / OmniShader](2511-multi-layer-shading-omnishader.md) is a technique for volume rendering that computes eight distinct visualization layers simultaneously in a single forward pass, returning them as a tuple of tensors. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Distance Color Blending](791-distance-color-blending.md) or [Ray-marched Shadows](1087-ray-marched-shadows.md) by decoupling geometric, photometric, and stylistic attributes into parallel outputs rather than combining them into a single RGB image, allowing for flexible post-processing or neural network ingestion.
 
 ### [Spherical Gaussian Shading](3169-spherical-gaussian-shading.md)
-Approximates complex lighting environments and specular reflections by decomposing incident radiance into a sum of Spherical Gaussians, which are then rendered as discrete point lights or integrated into a microfacet BRDF model, solving the problem of real-time or efficient relighting in scenes with spatially variant lighting. It explicitly models specular highlights and view-dependent reflectance, differing from pure diffuse models by capturing view-dependent effects. Choose it for efficient, view-dependent specular shading in neural rendering or procedural scenes [G].
+[Spherical Gaussian Shading](3169-spherical-gaussian-shading.md) approximates complex lighting environments and specular reflections by decomposing incident radiance into a sum of Spherical Gaussians, which are then rendered as discrete point lights or integrated into a microfacet BRDF model. It solves the problem of efficient relighting in scenes with spatially variant lighting, such as Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs), by avoiding the high computational cost of full spherical harmonic integration. Unlike [Diffuse Lighting Models](5343-diffuse-lighting-models.md) which ignore view-dependent effects, SG Shading explicitly models specular highlights and view-dependent reflectance.
