 ## Background
-The integration of lighting models with Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) emerged alongside the popularization of ray marching for real-time rendering [G]. Early implementations relied on calculating surface normals using finite differences, specifically solving a linear system based on values at vertices of a tetrahedron, or via back-propagation [S1, unofficial]. The development of efficient ambient occlusion techniques specific to SDFs, such as the linear step with exponential decay method, allowed for local illumination effects without the overhead of global illumination algorithms [G]. More recent advancements include the use of Spherical Gaussians for efficient environment lighting approximation in neural rendering and procedural scenes [S1, unofficial], and custom shading pipelines for scientific visualization [S1, unofficial].
+The foundational concept of shading ray-marched surfaces relies on the ability to estimate normals from Signed Distance Functions, a technique popularized in real-time graphics by tools like [GLSL SDF](https://iquilezles.org/articles/) by Inigo Quilez [S1, unofficial]. The specific implementations cited here, such as the Neon Raymarcher and OmniShader, originate from modern real-time rendering experiments and open-source raymarching systems [S1, unofficial]. The use of Spherical Gaussians for relighting in neural radiance fields represents a more recent development in the intersection of implicit surfaces and neural rendering [S2].
 
